---
title: Roast chicken
description: Lesson 2.2. Salting a day ahead, roasting to a temperature, and resting. Start the day before.
servings: 4
time: 1 hour 40 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, the-oven]
lesson: "2.2"
technique: dry brining and roasting
status: draft
---

> Technique: salting ahead, often called dry brining. Salt goes on the
> bird a day early, so it has time to travel into the meat. Then the
> chicken is roasted to a temperature, not a time, and rested.

The day before, pat a @whole chicken{1}(about 1.6 kg) dry with kitchen paper. Loosen the skin over the breast with your fingers and rub about a third of @fine salt{12%g} onto the meat underneath. Rub the rest over the outside and inside the cavity.

> Why under the skin, and why a day: salt moves into cold food slowly,
> and skin keeps it out. America's Test Kitchen compares the skin to a
> raincoat.
-- source: "How to Season Smarter with Salt", America's Test Kitchen, 2023-11-01, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/7476-how-to-season-smarter-with-salt

Set the chicken on a #rack{} over a #roasting tin{} and leave it in the fridge, uncovered, until tomorrow.

> Science: salt changes how muscle holds water. Muscle fibres are
> bundles of protein filaments, the myofibrils. In salt solution the
> chloride ions bind to the filaments and push them apart, and the
> lattice swells, to about twice its volume in the laboratory, holding
> more water. America's Test Kitchen describes the same thing as
> proteins unwinding and tangling into a matrix that traps water. Look
> up: myofibril, water-holding capacity, dry brining.
-- source: Offer and Trusler, "On the mechanism of water holding in meat: The swelling and shrinking of myofibrils", Meat Science 8(4):245-281, 1983, https://doi.org/10.1016/0309-1740(83)90013-X (from the abstract as indexed; the paper is paywalled and was not read in full) ; "The Science of Brining", America's Test Kitchen, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/how_tos/8243-the-science-of-brining

Heat the oven to 220 °C (200 °C fan). Roast the chicken, breast side up, for about ~{65%minutes}, until the thickest part of the thigh, away from the bone, reads at least 74 °C on a #thermometer{}.

> Safety: 74 °C is the safe minimum for all poultry, read before the
> bird leaves the oven. Check both thighs.
-- source: USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Safe Minimum Internal Temperature Chart", last updated 2025-04-14 (read from an archive copy), https://www.fsis.usda.gov/food-safety/safe-food-handling-and-preparation/food-safety-basics/safe-temperature-chart : all poultry 165 °F (73.9 °C)

Rest the chicken on a board, uncovered, for ~{15%minutes} before carving.

> Why: a large roast keeps climbing after it leaves the oven, by about
> 6 to 8 °C in kitchen measurements, so it is still cooking on the
> board.
-- source: Meathead Goldwyn with Greg Blonder, "The Science of Resting Meat", AmazingRibs.com, https://amazingribs.com/more-technique-and-science/more-cooking-science/science-of-resting-meat/ (carryover of roughly 10 to 15 °F for a large roast)

> Next time, without the recipe: salt any bird a day ahead, under the
> skin as well as on it, and leave it uncovered in the fridge. Roast
> it hot, stop at 74 °C in the thigh, and rest it before you cut.
